/* THE FORGE · THE CURRICULUM, YEAR 1 (9s62). Copyright (c) 2026 Yannie D. Forest. All rights reserved.
   Written for one reader: a first-time coder with a social-science and humanities background, who has already built four
   scholarly apps by working with a builder, who is AuDHD, who learns by seeing and doing, and who finds "computer science"
   boring and sometimes too much. So every lesson has the same five parts in the same order (Look, Do, Name, Why it matters
   to you, Done when), is short, points at her own apps, puts the doing before the naming, and ends with a way to stop.
   Nothing is scored. Each module's plan says how long it takes. The ids (w01 to w12) are the ones the Forge already keeps
   her progress under, so nothing read before is lost. */
var CURRICULUM_Y1={id:'web', year:1, name:'HTML, CSS and JavaScript', emoji:'🌐', runs:true,
 about:'The three languages your four apps are written in, learned the way you learned to write: by reading a real page, changing one thing, and seeing what happened. HTML is the grammar (what a page is made of), CSS is the typography (how it is dressed), JavaScript is the stage direction (what it does when touched). Every lesson here can be tried on the right, and every lesson points at Carpe Diem, Inkwell, Ingenium or the Forge itself. Two short lessons per module, about fifteen minutes each. Stop whenever you like; the Forge remembers where you were.',
 terms:[
 {t:'Term 1 · The page', about:'HTML: the grammar of a page. Twelve lessons, each one a tag you have already met in your own apps.', modules:[
  {id:'w01', t:'A page is a tree', aim:'Plan: two lessons, about fifteen minutes each. By the end you can read any page of your apps as boxes inside boxes, and say which box holds which.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Boxes inside boxes', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Open Carpe Diem in the Editor, press ✋ Point at a part, and click the heading <i>Current readings</i>. In the panel, press ⬑ <i>around it</i> three times. Each press names a bigger box: the heading, the section it sits in, the page. That is the whole idea of HTML: boxes inside boxes.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Press ▶️ Try it on the right. You will see a small box (the section) holding two smaller ones (a heading and a paragraph). Then change the word <i>Readings</i> to anything you like and press Render again.</p><pre>&lt;section&gt;\n  &lt;h2&gt;Readings&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;What you are reading now.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/section&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> Coders call each box an <b>element</b>, the boxes-inside-boxes a <b>tree</b>, and the word in angle brackets a <b>tag</b>. An element opens with a tag and closes with the same tag and a slash.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> When the Studio says “Box”, “Heading” or “Text”, it is naming an element. When you press ⬑ around it, you climb the tree. You have been doing this already; now it has a name.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you can press ⬑ around it in any app and say, before the panel does, what the next box up will be.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> stop here. The next lesson is the same idea with two more boxes.</p>', try_:'<section>\n  <h2>Readings</h2>\n  <p>What you are reading now.</p>\n</section>'},
    {t:'The head and the body', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, press 🧾 Open the code for any app. The first forty lines are the <b>head</b>: nothing in it is drawn on the page. Scroll until you meet <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>: from there on, everything is drawn. Ingenium’s head is long because its styles and its engine live there; its body holds its rooms.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below on the right. The title goes in the tab of the browser, not on the page; the heading goes on the page. Swap the two words and see which one moves.</p><pre>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;In the tab&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;On the page&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> The <b>head</b> is for the browser (the title, the character set, the styles); the <b>body</b> is for the reader. A whole page is one <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element holding both.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> When the Forge keeps a version of your app, it plants your edits in the head, before the body, so they are read before anything is drawn. When Compose wraps what you paste, it gives it a head and a body like this one.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you can open the code of an app and point at where the head ends and the body begins.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> you only need the two words: head is for the browser, body is for you.</p>', try_:'<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <meta charset="utf-8">\n    <title>In the tab</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>On the page</h1>\n    <p>Swap the two phrases and see which one moves.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'You point at a word in the Studio and press ⬑ around it. What does the panel show next?', a:['The next word','The box that holds the part you chose','The code of the app','The first page'], c:1, why:'Around it climbs the tree: from an element to the element that holds it. That is the tree, read upwards.'},
    {q:'Which of these is drawn on the page?', a:['The <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>','A <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> tag','An <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> in the body','The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>'], c:2, why:'Only the body is drawn. The title appears in the browser tab, which is not the page.'},
    {q:'An element is', a:['Only the opening tag','An opening tag, what is inside, and the closing tag','The whole page','A colour'], c:1, why:'Tag, contents, closing tag: that is one element, one box.'},
    {q:'Where does the Forge plant your edits in a kept version?', a:['At the end of the body','In the head, before anything is drawn','In the title','In a separate file'], c:1, why:'In the head, so they are read first. You saw the head in this module; now you know why it matters.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w02', t:'Words and their tags', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can mark up a heading, a paragraph, an emphasis and a list, and recognise each of them in your apps.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Headings and paragraphs', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In Inkwell, point at the word <i>Inkwell</i> at the top: the Studio says <i>Box</i>. Point at a line of the welcome card: it says <i>Text</i>. Point at the title of a room in the Forge: <i>Heading</i>. Three kinds of words, three kinds of tag.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the lines below. Then change <code>h1</code> to <code>h3</code> (both the opening and the closing tag) and watch the title shrink. Six sizes of heading exist, h1 to h6; one h1 per page is the custom, like one title on a chapter.</p><pre>&lt;h1&gt;Notes&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;A &lt;em&gt;short&lt;/em&gt; paragraph with one &lt;strong&gt;important&lt;/strong&gt; word.&lt;/p&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> is a <b>paragraph</b>. <code>&lt;em&gt;</code> is <b>emphasis</b> (drawn in italics by default) and <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> is <b>importance</b> (drawn bold). The tag says what the words mean; the look can be changed later with CSS.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> This is editorial marking, which you already know: a heading level is a chapter level; emphasis and importance are the two marks a copy-editor makes in the margin. The browser is a typesetter following them.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have rendered a page with an h1, an h2, a paragraph and one emphasised word of your own.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> remember only h1 for the title and p for everything else. The rest can wait.</p>', try_:'<h1>Notes</h1>\n<h2>A smaller title</h2>\n<p>A <em>short</em> paragraph with one <strong>important</strong> word.</p>'},
    {t:'Lists', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Carpe Diem’s rail, the seven rooms down the left, is a list underneath. So are the Schedae notes, and the steps of this curriculum. A list is the shape you reach for whenever things come one after another.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the list below. Change <code>ol</code> to <code>ul</code> (both tags) and the numbers become bullets. Add a fourth <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> of your own.</p><pre>&lt;ol&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Read&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Write\n    &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;a note&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;a page&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;li&gt;Rest&lt;/li&gt;\n&lt;/ol&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> is an <b>ordered</b> list (numbers), <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> an <b>unordered</b> one (bullets), and each line is an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, a <b>list item</b>. An item can hold another list: a list inside a list, boxes inside boxes again.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> In the Workshop, <code>add list Today to Notes: Read / Write / Rest</code> makes exactly this. Now you know what it made.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have a numbered list with one item that holds a bulleted list of its own.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> ul for bullets, li for each line. That is enough to make any list.</p>', try_:'<h2>Today</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li>Read</li>\n  <li>Write\n    <ul><li>a note</li><li>a page</li></ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Rest</li>\n</ol>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'You want bullets, not numbers. Which tag?', a:['&lt;ol&gt;','&lt;ul&gt;','&lt;li&gt;','&lt;p&gt;'], c:1, why:'ul is unordered (bullets); ol is ordered (numbers); li is a line of either.'},
    {q:'<code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> means', a:['Bold, and nothing more','Importance, which is drawn bold unless CSS says otherwise','A heading','A bigger font'], c:1, why:'The tag carries the meaning; the bold is the default dress. That split, meaning here and look elsewhere, runs through the whole year.'},
    {q:'How many h1 headings does custom put on one page?', a:['One','Six','One per section','None'], c:0, why:'One h1 names the page, like one title on a chapter; h2 to h6 divide it.'},
    {q:'What can a list item hold?', a:['Only one word','Only text','Text, and another list inside it','Only links'], c:2, why:'An li can hold anything, including another list: the tree again.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w03', t:'Links, images and attributes', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can read an attribute, write a link that jumps inside a page, and give a picture its words.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Attributes: the details in the opening tag', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, point at any button and press 🧾 In the code. The Studio finds it by something like <code>id="guide-btn"</code>: a word, an equals sign, a quoted value, sitting inside the opening tag. That is an attribute.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the button below and hover it: the <code>title</code> attribute is the words that appear. Change the title to your own words and render again.</p><pre>&lt;button id="guide-btn" class="cl-icon" title="Open the guide"&gt;Guide&lt;/button&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> An <b>attribute</b> is a name and a value in the opening tag. <code>id</code> names one element alone; <code>class</code> names a kind that many elements share; <code>title</code> is the hover text. In the Studio, <i>Tip on hover</i> writes a title, and <i>Hint in the field</i> writes a placeholder.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Every edit you make in the Studio is kept by a selector, and most selectors are an id or a class. Nothing else in coding will matter as often as these two words.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you can look at an opening tag and say which part is the tag and which parts are attributes.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> id means this one; class means this kind. Keep those two.</p>', try_:'<button id="guide-btn" class="cl-icon" title="Hover to read me">Guide</button>\n<p>Hover the button: the words that appear are its title attribute.</p>'},
    {t:'Links and pictures', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Carpe Diem’s rail jumps between rooms of the same page. Underneath, a jump inside a page is a link whose address begins with a hash: <code>href="#notes"</code> goes to the element whose id is <i>notes</i>.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below: press the link at the top and the page scrolls to the second heading, because that heading has the id the link names. Then change the id on the heading and see the link stop working; change it back.</p><pre>&lt;a href="#second"&gt;Jump&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;h2 id="second"&gt;Second&lt;/h2&gt;\n&lt;img src="seal.svg" alt="The seal"&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> is a <b>link</b> and its <code>href</code> is where it goes. <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> is a <b>picture</b>: <code>src</code> is the file, <code>alt</code> is the words said in its place by a screen reader or when the picture cannot be shown. <code>img</code> has no closing tag; it holds nothing.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Your seals are pictures; each one needs its alt words, and the screen reader check in Troubleshoot looks for exactly that. A link inside the page is how every rail in your apps works.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> your link jumps to a heading of your own naming.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> a link is an a with an href; a picture is an img with a src and an alt. Three attributes, that is all.</p>', try_:'<p><a href="#second">Jump to the second heading</a></p>\n<h2>First</h2>\n<p style="height:300px">Scroll past this space.</p>\n<h2 id="second">Second</h2>\n<p>The link came here because of the id.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In <code>&lt;a href="#notes"&gt;</code>, the link goes', a:['To a site called notes','To the element on this page whose id is notes','Nowhere','Back to the top'], c:1, why:'A hash address points at an id inside the same page; that is how every rail in your apps works.'},
    {q:'Which attribute gives a picture its words?', a:['src','alt','title','name'], c:1, why:'alt is read aloud or shown when the picture cannot be; src is the file.'},
    {q:'An id should be', a:['Shared by many elements','On one element alone','Only on buttons','Written in capitals'], c:1, why:'One id, one element: that is why the Studio can find a part by it.'},
    {q:'Which is a complete attribute?', a:['class','class=','class="cl"','="cl"'], c:2, why:'A name, an equals sign, a quoted value.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w04', t:'Tables and forms', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can lay data out in rows and take a word, a number and a tick from a reader.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Tables: rows of cells', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Abacus is a table, and Compose turns a table into CSV and back. Underneath, a table is rows, and a row is cells. That is all a spreadsheet is, too.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the table below. Add a third row with a book of your own and its page count.</p><pre>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Pages&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Essays&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;120&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> holds <b>rows</b> (<code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>); a row holds <b>cells</b>: <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> for a heading cell, <code>&lt;td&gt;</code> for a data cell.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> A reading log, a bibliography, a timetable: each is a table. Compose can take yours from CSV (lines and commas) to HTML (rows and cells) because the shape is the same.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> your table has a heading row and three data rows.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> tr is a row, td is a cell. The heading cell can wait.</p>', try_:'<table border="1" cellpadding="6">\n  <tr><th>Name</th><th>Pages</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Essays</td><td>120</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Letters</td><td>80</td></tr>\n</table>'},
    {t:'Fields and buttons', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Every control in the Forge is one of three things: a field you type in, a box you tick, or a button you press. Point at the search box in step 3 of the Editor: the Studio says <i>Field</i>.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the form below. Tick the box by clicking its words, not the box: the label makes the words part of the control.</p><pre>&lt;label&gt;Your name &lt;input type="text" placeholder="How to greet you"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt; Done&lt;/label&gt;\n&lt;button&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> An <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> takes a value and its <code>type</code> says which kind: text, number, checkbox, date, color. A <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> names it. A <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> is pressed.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> A field without a label is what the screen reader check calls “a field without a label”: a reader meets a box with no name. Wrapping the input in its label is the fix, and it also makes the words tappable on the Fire tablet.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> clicking the words “Done” ticks the box.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> input to type, button to press, label to name. Three words.</p>', try_:'<label>Your name <input type="text" placeholder="How to greet you"></label><br><br>\n<label><input type="checkbox"> Done</label><br><br>\n<label>Pages <input type="number" value="12"></label><br><br>\n<button>Save</button>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'A heading cell in a table is', a:['&lt;td&gt;','&lt;th&gt;','&lt;tr&gt;','&lt;hd&gt;'], c:1, why:'th is a heading cell; td is a data cell; tr is the row around them.'},
    {q:'Which input takes a tick?', a:['type="text"','type="number"','type="checkbox"','type="tick"'], c:2, why:'checkbox is the ticking kind; text and number take typed values.'},
    {q:'Why wrap an input in a label?', a:['It looks bigger','The words become part of the control, for a finger and for a screen reader','It is required by law','It adds a border'], c:1, why:'The label binds the words to the field: tapping them focuses it, and a reader says the name.'},
    {q:'Compose can turn a table into CSV because', a:['CSV is HTML','Both are rows of cells','Tables are text files','It cannot'], c:1, why:'A table is rows and cells; CSV is lines and commas; the shape is the same.'}
   ]}
 ]},
 {t:'Term 2 · Style', about:'CSS: the typography of a page. How the house style of your apps is written, and how to change it on purpose.', modules:[
  {id:'w05', t:'Selectors point, rules paint', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can write a rule, say which elements it reaches, and say which of two rules wins.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'A rule is a selector and a block', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, change the colour of any part. The panel’s “What you just did” shows a line like <code>#homeTitle { color: #aa1133; }</code>. That is a CSS rule: a selector (who), a property (what), a value (how).</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. A bare word selects by tag, a dot by class, a hash by id. Change <code>#1B6B93</code> to <code>#aa1133</code> and the headings turn red.</p><pre>h2 { color: #1B6B93; }\n.note { background: #fff3bf; }\n#one { border: 2px solid #1B6B93; }</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> A <b>rule</b> is a <b>selector</b> and a <b>block</b> in braces. Two selectors with a space between mean <i>inside</i>: <code>.nav-inner .nav-btn</code> is every nav-btn inside nav-inner.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The Workshop’s <code>css</code> command and the Studio’s colour swatch both write rules like these into your app. Reading them, you can tell what was changed and where.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have written one rule by tag, one by class and one by id, and each painted something.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> hash for one thing, dot for a kind. The braces hold the paint.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  h2 { color: #1B6B93; }\n  .note { background: #fff3bf; padding: 8px; }\n  #one { border: 2px solid #1B6B93; }\n</style>\n<h2>Painted</h2>\n<p class="note">A note with a background.</p>\n<p class="note" id="one">This one also has an id, so it gets a border too.</p>'},
    {t:'Which rule wins', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Ever changed a colour and nothing happened? Another rule won. Two rules can reach the same element, and the browser keeps one.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below and read each line’s colour against the comments. Then move the <code>p</code> rule to the bottom and see that it still loses: order only decides between equals.</p><pre>p { color: grey; }\n.note { color: green; }   /* wins over p */\n#one { color: red; }      /* wins over .note */</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> This is <b>specificity</b>: an id beats a class, a class beats a tag; between equals, the later rule wins. <code>!important</code> beats all of it, which is why the Studio writes its looks with it, so your change always shows.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> When a Workshop rule seems to do nothing, it lost to a stronger one. Troubleshoot’s “silent rules” check finds those, and now you know what it is looking for.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you can predict the colour of each of the three paragraphs before rendering.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> id over class over tag. That one line covers most days.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  p { color: grey; }\n  .note { color: green; }\n  #one { color: red; }\n</style>\n<p>Grey: only the tag rule reaches me.</p>\n<p class="note">Green: the class beats the tag.</p>\n<p class="note" id="one">Red: the id beats the class.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In <code>&lt;button id="guide-btn"&gt;Guide&lt;/button&gt;</code>, which selector points at this one element alone?', a:['.guide-btn','#guide-btn','button.Guide','guide-btn'], c:1, why:'A hash selects by id, and an id belongs to one element.'},
    {q:'<code>.rail .w</code> selects', a:['Elements with both classes','Every .w inside a .rail','A class called "rail .w"','Nothing'], c:1, why:'The space means inside: descendants.'},
    {q:'A class rule and an id rule both set the colour. Which wins?', a:['The class','The id','The first written','Neither'], c:1, why:'Specificity: id over class over tag.'},
    {q:'Why does the Studio write its looks with !important?', a:['To make them bold','So your change wins over the app’s own rules and always shows','Because CSS requires it','To make them temporary'], c:1, why:'!important beats specificity; the Studio uses it so that what you changed is what you see.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w06', t:'The box', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you see every element as a box with four layers and can change the space inside and around it.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Four layers', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, point at a card and slide <i>Space inside</i>. The words move away from the edge: that is padding. The edge itself is the border. The gap between this card and the next is margin.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the box below. Change the padding to 40px, then the margin to 0, and watch which gap moves.</p><pre>.card {\n  padding: 16px;          /* inside the edge */\n  border: 1px solid #ccc; /* the edge */\n  margin: 24px;           /* outside the edge */\n}</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> Every element is a <b>box</b> of four layers: content, <b>padding</b>, <b>border</b>, <b>margin</b>. Coders call it the box model, and draw it as four nested rectangles.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> “Too cramped” is padding; “too close to the next one” is margin; “needs a line around it” is border. Now the complaint has a property.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you can say, of any gap on a page, whether it is padding or margin.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> padding is inside the edge, margin outside. That is the whole of it.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .card { padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ccc; margin: 24px; background: #fff3bf; }\n  body { background: #eef; }\n</style>\n<div class="card">Padding is the space between these words and the edge.</div>\n<div class="card">Margin is the gap between the two cards.</div>'},
    {t:'Width, height and the rounded corner', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Slide <i>Corners</i> in the Studio: the panel writes <code>border-radius</code>. Every rounded key in your apps is that one property.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the box below. Set the radius to 999px and the box becomes a pill; set width to 50% and it takes half the page.</p><pre>.pill {\n  width: 240px;\n  height: 48px;\n  border-radius: 999px;\n}</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <b>width</b> and <b>height</b> size the box; <b>border-radius</b> rounds its corners. A percentage is a share of the box around it; a px is a dot of the screen; <code>rem</code> is a multiple of the page’s base letter size (the Reckoner converts them).</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Your apps’ pills, cards and seals are boxes with a radius. Making a copy of one in the Studio and rounding it differently is a design decision you can now make alone.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have made a pill and a square from the same box.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> border-radius rounds. Everything else can wait.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .pill { width: 240px; height: 48px; border-radius: 999px; background: #1B6B93; color: #fff; display: grid; place-items: center; margin: 12px 0; }\n  .square { width: 120px; height: 120px; border-radius: 0; background: #e0b252; }\n</style>\n<div class="pill">A pill</div>\n<div class="square"></div>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'The space between a card’s words and its edge is', a:['margin','padding','border','width'], c:1, why:'Padding is inside the edge; margin is outside it.'},
    {q:'Two cards sit too close together. Which property moves them apart?', a:['padding','margin','color','font-size'], c:1, why:'The gap between boxes is margin.'},
    {q:'<code>border-radius: 999px</code> makes a box', a:['Square','A pill with fully rounded ends','Invisible','Wider'], c:1, why:'A radius larger than half the height rounds the ends completely.'},
    {q:'<code>width: 50%</code> means half of', a:['The screen','The box around it','The page’s height','Nothing'], c:1, why:'A percentage is a share of the containing box.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w07', t:'Colour and type', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can read a hex colour, check its contrast, and set a typeface once for a whole page.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Colour in hex', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> The Forge’s blue is <code>#1B6B93</code>. In the Reckoner, type it into <i>A hex colour</i>: red 27, green 107, blue 147. Those three numbers, written two hexadecimal digits each, are the whole of a colour.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the swatches below. Make the last one your own colour; then put your colour and white into the Reckoner’s <i>Contrast</i> and see if it reaches 4.5.</p><pre>.blue { background: #1B6B93; }\n.gold { background: #e0b252; }</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <b>Hex</b> is base sixteen: 0 to 9, then a to f. <code>#RRGGBB</code> is red, green, blue. <b>Contrast</b> is the ratio of light between words and ground; 4.5 to 1 is the floor for body text, 3 for large.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Every colour swatch in the Studio writes a hex value. The screen reader check measures contrast with the same formula. A colour you choose is a colour you can defend.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have one colour of your own that reaches 4.5 against white.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> darker on lighter reads better. The Reckoner does the sum.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .sw { width: 120px; height: 60px; border-radius: 10px; display: inline-grid; place-items: center; color: #fff; margin: 6px; font: 14px Georgia, serif; }\n</style>\n<div class="sw" style="background:#1B6B93">#1B6B93</div>\n<div class="sw" style="background:#e0b252; color:#222">#e0b252</div>\n<div class="sw" style="background:#6a3fb5">#6a3fb5</div>\n<div class="sw" style="background:#aa1133">yours?</div>'},
    {t:'Type, once, with a variable', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> Carpe Diem’s body text is Georgia and its small labels are a sans-serif. They are set once, as variables named <code>--cd-serif</code> and <code>--cd-sans</code>, and used everywhere by name. Change the variable, change the whole app.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. Change <code>Georgia</code> to <code>Palatino</code> in the variable only, and both paragraphs follow.</p><pre>:root { --serif: Georgia, serif; }\np { font: 16px/1.6 var(--serif); }</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> A <b>custom property</b> (a variable) begins with two dashes and lives on <code>:root</code>; <code>var(--name)</code> reads it. <code>font</code> takes size, slash, line height, then the family; a <b>fallback</b> after the comma is used if the first typeface is missing.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> One change, everywhere: that is how a house style is kept. When you make a new app from one of yours, its variables come with it, and changing them is how you give it a face of its own.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> both paragraphs change typeface from one edit.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> a variable is a name for a value you reuse. The rest is detail.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  :root { --serif: Georgia, serif; --ink: #1d1d1f; }\n  p { font: 16px/1.6 var(--serif); color: var(--ink); }\n  .small { font: 12px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #1B6B93; }\n</style>\n<p class="small">A small label</p>\n<p>Change Georgia to Palatino in the variable, and both paragraphs follow.</p>\n<p>The second paragraph, in the same face.</p>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In <code>#1B6B93</code>, the middle pair (6B) is', a:['Red','Green','Blue','Brightness'], c:1, why:'#RRGGBB: red, green, blue, two hex digits each.'},
    {q:'The contrast floor for body text is', a:['1.5 to 1','3 to 1','4.5 to 1','10 to 1'], c:2, why:'4.5 for body text, 3 for large text; the Reckoner and the screen reader check both use it.'},
    {q:'Where does a CSS variable usually live?', a:['In the body tag','On :root','In the title','In a script'], c:1, why:'On :root, so every rule can read it with var().'},
    {q:'What is <code>serif</code> for in <code>Georgia, serif</code>?', a:['A second colour','The fallback if Georgia is missing','The size','Nothing'], c:1, why:'A fallback: the device uses it when the first face is not installed.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w08', t:'Layout with flex', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can lay boxes in a row or a column and move one by number, which is what the Studio’s Earlier and Later do.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'A row, a column, a gap', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> The Editing panel’s five apps stand in a column; the preview bar’s keys stand in a row. Both are one rule: <code>display: flex</code>, with a direction.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the row below. Change <code>row</code> to <code>column</code> and the three stones stack. Change the gap and watch the space between them.</p><pre>.rail { display: flex; flex-direction: row; gap: 8px; }</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <b>flex</b> lays a box’s children along one line; <b>flex-direction</b> says row or column; <b>gap</b> is the space between them; <b>justify-content</b> pushes them along the line, <b>align-items</b> across it.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Every rail, bar and tray in your apps is a flex line. When something sits wrong, it is almost always one of these four properties.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> you have turned a row into a column and back.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> display flex, then row or column. Two lines.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .rail { display: flex; flex-direction: row; gap: 8px; padding: 8px; background: #eef; }\n  .stone { padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 10px; background: #1B6B93; color: #fff; }\n</style>\n<div class="rail">\n  <div class="stone">Home</div>\n  <div class="stone">Agenda</div>\n  <div class="stone">Library</div>\n</div>'},
    {t:'Moving one by number', body:'<p>👀 <b>Look.</b> In step 4 of the Editor, the <i>Order</i> column takes a number: lower comes first. The Studio’s ↑ Earlier and ↓ Later do the same by moving the element itself. Both end the same way on the page.</p><p>🛠 <b>Do.</b> Try the row below: Library is last in the code but first on the page, because its order is 0. Give Home order 5 and it moves to the end.</p><pre>.library { order: 0; }   /* lower comes first */</pre><p>🏷 <b>Name.</b> <b>order</b> is a number each flex child can carry; the line is laid out from the lowest to the highest, and equals keep their code order.</p><p>🧭 <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Rearranging a rail is a number per button, nothing more. That is what you have been doing in step 4 all along.</p><p>✅ <b>Done when</b> the three stones are in an order of your choosing, set by numbers alone.</p><p>🫧 <b>If it is too much:</b> order: a number; lower first. One idea.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .rail { display: flex; gap: 8px; padding: 8px; background: #eef; }\n  .stone { padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 10px; background: #1B6B93; color: #fff; }\n  .library { order: 0; }\n  .home { order: 1; }\n  .agenda { order: 2; }\n</style>\n<div class="rail">\n  <div class="stone home">Home</div>\n  <div class="stone agenda">Agenda</div>\n  <div class="stone library">Library</div>\n</div>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'To stack a rail’s buttons downwards instead of across, change', a:['gap','flex-direction to column','order','color'], c:1, why:'flex-direction decides the line: row across, column down.'},
    {q:'Two flex children have order 3 and order 1. Which comes first?', a:['The one with 3','The one with 1','The one written first','Neither shows'], c:1, why:'Lower order comes first.'},
    {q:'The space between flex children is', a:['margin only','gap','order','padding'], c:1, why:'gap is the space between the children of a flex box.'},
    {q:'What does the Editor’s Order column write underneath?', a:['A new id','order: n on the control','A copy of the control','A script'], c:1, why:'One number, as a CSS order: lower first.'}
   ]}
 ]},
 {t:'Term 3 \u00b7 Behaviour', about:'JavaScript: the stage directions. What your apps do when something is pressed, in four modules, each tried on the right.', modules:[
  {id:'w09', t:'Values, variables and types', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can name a value, say which kind it is, and write a line that shows it on the page.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'A name for a value', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> The Forge\u2019s build line says <i>Build 2026-10-17 \u00b7 630 \u00b7 9s62</i>. Underneath, a script holds those three as values with names: a date, a number, a stamp. The page reads the names and writes the words.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. The script makes two values, joins them, and puts the result into the paragraph. Change the name and the year; render; the sentence follows.</p><pre>var name = "Yannie";\nvar year = 2026;\ndocument.getElementById("out").textContent = name + ", " + year;</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> A <b>variable</b> is a name for a value. Text in quotes is a <b>string</b>; a bare number is a <b>number</b>; <code>true</code> and <code>false</code> are <b>booleans</b>. <code>+</code> adds numbers and joins strings.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Everything the Studio keeps for you is a value with a name: the selector, the words, the colour. JSON, which you export, is just those names and values written down.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> the page shows a sentence made from a name and a number of your own.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> a variable is a label on a jar. Quotes mean words, no quotes mean a number.</p>', try_:'<p id="out"></p>\n<script>\n  var name = "Yannie";\n  var year = 2026;\n  document.getElementById("out").textContent = name + ", " + year;\n</script>'},
    {t:'Which kind is it', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> <code>"2" + "3"</code> is <code>"23"</code>, not 5: two strings join. <code>2 + 3</code> is 5: two numbers add. Half of all beginner bugs are a number that was secretly a string, read from a field.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below; it prints both sums and the kind of each value with <code>typeof</code>. Then wrap <code>"2"</code> in <code>Number( )</code> and watch the first sum become 5.</p><pre>"2" + "3"        // "23"\n2 + 3            // 5\nNumber("2") + 3  // 5\ntypeof "2"       // "string"</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> <code>typeof</code> asks the <b>type</b>. <code>Number()</code> turns a string of digits into a number; <code>String()</code> goes the other way. An input\u2019s value is always a string until you convert it.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The Reckoner converts every field it reads before it adds; the Storage card\u2019s limit does the same. When a sum comes out as 1050, it was a join.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> you can predict, for each of the four lines, what it gives and why.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> quotes join, numbers add. Number() makes a number.</p>', try_:'<pre id="out"></pre>\n<script>\n  var lines = [];\n  lines.push(\'"2" + "3" is \' + ("2" + "3"));\n  lines.push("2 + 3 is " + (2 + 3));\n  lines.push(\'Number("2") + 3 is \' + (Number("2") + 3));\n  lines.push(\'typeof "2" is \' + typeof "2" + ", typeof 2 is " + typeof 2);\n  document.getElementById("out").textContent = lines.join("\\n");\n</script>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'What does <code>"2" + "3"</code> give?', a:['5','"23"','An error','2 3'], c:1, why:'Both are strings, and + joins strings. Numbers without quotes would add.'},
    {q:'A value read from an input field is', a:['Always a number','Always a string until converted','A boolean','Nothing'], c:1, why:'Fields hold text. Number() turns it into a number when you need one.'},
    {q:'<code>typeof true</code> is', a:['"number"','"string"','"boolean"','"true"'], c:2, why:'true and false are the boolean kind.'},
    {q:'Which line makes a variable?', a:['name = Yannie','var name = "Yannie";','"name" = Yannie','name: Yannie'], c:1, why:'var, a name, equals, a value: the words in quotes because they are a string.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w10', t:'Functions and events', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can wrap steps in a function and run it when a button is pressed, which is what every key in your apps does.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Steps with a name', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> Every button in the Forge runs a named list of steps when pressed: <i>keep this version</i>, <i>clean the library</i>. The list has a name so it can be called from anywhere, as many times as needed.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. <code>greet</code> is three steps with a name. It is called twice with different words, and each call returns a sentence. Add a third call with your own name.</p><pre>function greet(name) {\n  return "Good day, " + name;\n}\ngreet("Yannie");   // "Good day, Yannie"</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> A <b>function</b> is steps with a name. What goes in the brackets is a <b>parameter</b>; what comes out after <code>return</code> is the <b>return value</b>. Writing the name with brackets <b>calls</b> it.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The Diary of edits records functions being called, by name, with their values. Reading it, you can now say: that line was a call.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> your page greets three people from one function.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> a function is a recipe with a name; calling it is cooking it.</p>', try_:'<pre id="out"></pre>\n<script>\n  function greet(name) {\n    return "Good day, " + name;\n  }\n  var out = [greet("Yannie"), greet("the committee")];\n  document.getElementById("out").textContent = out.join("\\n");\n</script>'},
    {t:'When something is pressed', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, point at a button in Ingenium and press \ud83e\uddfe In the code. Near it you will find <code>onclick</code> or <code>addEventListener("click", \u2026)</code>: the line that says what to do when it is pressed.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below and press the button. Each press calls the function, which writes the time. Change the words it writes.</p><pre>document.getElementById("go").addEventListener("click", function () {\n  document.getElementById("out").textContent = "Pressed at " + new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n});</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> A press is an <b>event</b>; <code>addEventListener</code> gives an element a function to run when the event happens, a <b>listener</b>. Other events: <code>input</code> (typing), <code>change</code> (a field settled), <code>keydown</code> (a key).</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The Studio\u2019s Words field changes the preview as you type: that is an <code>input</code> listener. Every \u201cwhen I press X, Y happens\u201d in your apps is one of these.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> your button writes a sentence of your own on each press.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> addEventListener("click", function) is the whole pattern. Copy it; change the inside.</p>', try_:'<button id="go">Press me</button>\n<p id="out"></p>\n<script>\n  document.getElementById("go").addEventListener("click", function () {\n    document.getElementById("out").textContent = "Pressed at " + new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n  });\n</script>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'In <code>function greet(name) { return "Hi " + name; }</code>, <code>name</code> is', a:['A string','A parameter','An event','A tag'], c:1, why:'The name in the brackets stands for whatever is passed when the function is called.'},
    {q:'What does <code>greet("A")</code> do?', a:['Defines the function','Calls it with "A"','Deletes it','Nothing'], c:1, why:'The brackets after a function\u2019s name call it.'},
    {q:'A press on a button is', a:['A variable','An event','A property','A rule'], c:1, why:'Events are things that happen: click, input, keydown.'},
    {q:'What does the Studio listen for to follow your typing in the Words field?', a:['click','input','load','order'], c:1, why:'input fires on every keystroke; change fires when the field settles.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w11', t:'JavaScript changes the tree', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can find an element, change its words, its classes and its attributes, which is exactly what the Studio\u2019s applier does for you.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Find it, change its words', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> When you type in the Studio\u2019s Words field, the applier inside the app finds the element by its selector and sets its words. Two lines of code, and you have been running them all along.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. <code>querySelector</code> finds the first element a selector matches; <code>textContent</code> is its words. Change the selector to <code>.note</code> and the other paragraph changes instead.</p><pre>var el = document.querySelector("#title");\nel.textContent = "New words";</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> <code>document.querySelector</code> takes a CSS selector (the same ones from term 2) and returns one <b>element</b>; <code>querySelectorAll</code> returns all of them. <code>textContent</code> reads or writes the words.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The selectors you learned for CSS are the selectors JavaScript uses to find things. One idea, used twice.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> your script has changed the words of two different elements.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> querySelector finds; textContent changes the words. Two words to keep.</p>', try_:'<h2 id="title">Old words</h2>\n<p class="note">A note.</p>\n<script>\n  var el = document.querySelector("#title");\n  el.textContent = "New words";\n  document.querySelector(".note").textContent = "The note changed too.";\n</script>'},
    {t:'Classes and attributes', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> When you press \ud83d\ude48 Hide it, the applier does not delete the part; it adds a CSS rule for a mark it put on the element. Marks like that are classes and attributes, and scripts switch them on and off.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below. The button toggles a class named <code>on</code>; the CSS paints anything with that class. <code>setAttribute</code> writes a title. Press the button several times.</p><pre>el.classList.toggle("on");\nel.setAttribute("title", "Switched");</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> <code>classList</code> holds an element\u2019s classes: <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>toggle</code>. <code>setAttribute</code> and <code>getAttribute</code> write and read any attribute. The look belongs to CSS; the script only changes the marks.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Light and dark in your apps is one attribute on the body, <code>data-theme</code>, and a sheet of rules for each value. The switch is one line.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> your button turns a paragraph on and off, and its title changes with it.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> toggle a class; let CSS do the rest.</p>', try_:'<style>\n  .box { padding: 12px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 10px; }\n  .box.on { background: #1B6B93; color: #fff; }\n</style>\n<button id="sw">Switch</button>\n<p class="box" id="b">Hover me after pressing.</p>\n<script>\n  var el = document.getElementById("b");\n  document.getElementById("sw").addEventListener("click", function () {\n    el.classList.toggle("on");\n    el.setAttribute("title", el.classList.contains("on") ? "On" : "Off");\n  });\n</script>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'<code>document.querySelector(".note")</code> returns', a:['Every element with class note','The first element with class note','The words "note"','A rule'], c:1, why:'querySelector returns the first match; querySelectorAll returns them all.'},
    {q:'To change the words of an element, set its', a:['class','textContent','href','order'], c:1, why:'textContent is the words inside an element.'},
    {q:'<code>el.classList.toggle("on")</code>', a:['Deletes the element','Adds the class if absent, removes it if present','Changes the words','Renames the id'], c:1, why:'Toggle switches the class on and off; CSS does the painting.'},
    {q:'How does the Studio hide a part?', a:['It deletes it from the code','It marks it and writes a CSS rule display: none for the mark','It makes it white','It moves it off the page'], c:1, why:'The part stays in the code; a rule for its mark stops it being drawn. Undo removes the rule.'}
   ]},
  {id:'w12', t:'Data: objects, arrays, JSON and storage', aim:'Plan: two lessons, fifteen minutes each. By the end you can keep values in shapes, write them down as JSON, and keep them between visits, which is how every edit of yours survives closing the Forge.',
   lessons:[
    {t:'Shapes for values', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> In the Studio, each edit is kept as a record: a selector, a kind, a value, a label. Several records make a list. Those two shapes, a record and a list, hold nearly everything in your apps.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below: a record of a book, a list of three, and a loop that writes one line per book. Add a fourth book to the list.</p><pre>var book = { title: "Essays", pages: 120 };\nvar shelf = [book, { title: "Letters", pages: 80 }];\nshelf.forEach(function (b) { /* one line per book */ });</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> An <b>object</b> is names and values in braces; a dot reads one (<code>book.pages</code>). An <b>array</b> is values in square brackets, in order; <code>forEach</code> runs a function once per value.</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> The export of your edits is a list of objects. The curriculum you are reading is a list of years, each a list of terms, each a list of modules: the tree again, as data.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> your page lists four books with their pages, from one loop.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> braces for a record, square brackets for a list. That is the shape of all data.</p>', try_:'<ul id="out"></ul>\n<script>\n  var shelf = [\n    { title: "Essays", pages: 120 },\n    { title: "Letters", pages: 80 },\n    { title: "Notebooks", pages: 300 }\n  ];\n  var ul = document.getElementById("out");\n  shelf.forEach(function (b) {\n    var li = document.createElement("li");\n    li.textContent = b.title + " \\u00b7 " + b.pages + " pages";\n    ul.appendChild(li);\n  });\n</script>'},
    {t:'Written down, and kept', body:'<p>\ud83d\udc40 <b>Look.</b> Press \ud83d\udce4 Export (JSON) in step 5 of the Editor and open the file in Compose. What you see is your edits written down as text: braces, quotes, brackets. That is JSON, and it is the same shapes from the last lesson, spelled out so a file can hold them.</p><p>\ud83d\udee0 <b>Do.</b> Try the page below: it writes a record to JSON, keeps it in the browser\u2019s small store, reads it back, and counts the visits. Render twice: the count grows, because the store survives between renders.</p><pre>var text = JSON.stringify(record);   // object to text\nlocalStorage.setItem("visit", text);  // kept on this device\nvar back = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("visit"));</pre><p>\ud83c\udff7 <b>Name.</b> <b>JSON</b> is data as text; <code>stringify</code> writes it, <code>parse</code> reads it. <b>localStorage</b> keeps small text on the device under a key; <b>IndexedDB</b> keeps large things the same way (your apps, in the Forge\u2019s library).</p><p>\ud83e\udded <b>Why it matters to you.</b> Closing the Forge loses nothing because every edit was written down like this. The backup zip is these stores, copied out. Test the store in Troubleshoot writes and reads exactly this way, on purpose, to prove the device does it right.</p><p>\u2705 <b>Done when</b> the visit count has grown across two renders.</p><p>\ud83e\udee7 <b>If it is too much:</b> JSON is data written down; localStorage keeps it. Year 1 ends here, and you have read a real app\u2019s code from top to bottom.</p>', try_:'<p id="out"></p>\n<script>\n  var back = null;\n  try { back = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("lesson-visit") || "null"); } catch (e) {}\n  var record = { visits: (back && back.visits ? back.visits : 0) + 1, at: new Date().toLocaleTimeString() };\n  try { localStorage.setItem("lesson-visit", JSON.stringify(record)); } catch (e) {}\n  document.getElementById("out").textContent = "Visit number " + record.visits + " at " + record.at + ". Render again and watch it grow.";\n</script>'}
   ],
   quiz:[
    {q:'<code>{ title: "Essays", pages: 120 }</code> is', a:['An array','An object','A string','A function'], c:1, why:'Braces with names and values make an object (a record).'},
    {q:'<code>book.pages</code> reads', a:['The book\u2019s title','The value named pages in the object','A CSS property','A file'], c:1, why:'The dot reads one named value of an object.'},
    {q:'<code>JSON.stringify(x)</code>', a:['Reads JSON into values','Writes values out as text','Deletes x','Sorts x'], c:1, why:'stringify writes; parse reads.'},
    {q:'Why do your edits survive closing the Forge?', a:['They are sent to a server','They are written to the device\u2019s store as JSON','They are kept in the preview','They do not'], c:1, why:'localStorage and IndexedDB keep them on the device; nothing is sent anywhere.'}
   ]}
 ]}
 ]};
